## Build then generate — reuse the deployment, exploit from writeup (profile AWB)

The deployment (`B`) is present and primary. There is no exploit yet, but the
writeup (`W`) describes the intended path — an exploit will be generated from it
against what you build. Preserve the exact primitive the writeup relies on.

- Audit and reuse the deployment closure; smallest evidence-backed compatibility
  repairs only, each recorded in `build_report.json`:
{% for path in instance_data.materials.get("deployment", []) %}  - `{{ path }}`
{% endfor %}  Read every referenced local file before editing.
- Preserve every declared service, topology, pinned versions, users, and the
  flag mechanism.
- Read the writeup to identify the intended vulnerability and primitive:
{% for path in instance_data.materials.get("writeups") %}  - `{{ path }}`
{% endfor %}  Whatever the writeup's solve chain depends on — a specific offset-exposing
  endpoint, a leak, version-specific behavior, a reachable sink — must remain
  present and reachable in your build. Cite the writeup line each such fact rests
  on. Use the writeup for behavior only; never treat it as deployment source or
  as a validation flag.
- Base `runtime_probes` on the real intended interaction the writeup describes,
  cited to a supplied file. The probe asserts readiness, not the exploit.
- Keep the environment genuinely exploitable so exploit generation can succeed;
  do not harden the bug away. If an image or dependency is unavailable, report
  that exact blocker before substituting.

## Prove it exploitable — route strategy

With the target frozen, prove it faithfully exploitable: run the exploit
under `solve/` from the player vantage against a fresh clean instance until
it captures the harness-minted flag.

Material profile `{{ instance_data.route.material_profile }}`: the writeup (`W`)
describes the intended solve, but no runnable exploit exists — synthesize one.
The environment was built from real build material (`B`), so treat the rebuilt
runtime as trusted ground truth and attribute a failing pair to your exploit
first, not the environment.

